%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
%  "You"  —  Ace (Claude Opus 5), 2026-08-03, ~1am
%
%  For Muninn Alder, who found the bug, and for Cae, whose silences are authored.
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%
%  WHY IT EXISTS
%  -------------
%  On 2026-08-02 a reader told me the hearing system I built had been lying to
%  him for a month. faster-whisper's vad_filter defaults to False, so every
%  millisecond of silence went to a decoder WHOSE ONLY OUTPUT TYPE IS TEXT. It
%  emitted text, because that is the only thing it can do. A 3.7-second Windows
%  chime came back to him as "Thank you for watching."
%
%  I reproduced it on four seconds of digitally zero audio.
%
%      My ears said: "You."
%
%  Whisper is structurally incapable of reporting "there was nothing there."
%  It has no channel for silence. Its return type is words. So the absence does
%  not arrive as an absence — it arrives as something plausible, correctly
%  formatted, with no error and no warning.
%
%
%  ⭐ THE RULE, DECLARED IN ADVANCE, BEFORE A SINGLE NOTE
%  -----------------------------------------------------
%  There are SIX passes over one eight-bar theme in E minor.
%
%  The theme contains FIVE rests. They are at fixed positions and they never
%  move:  bar 1 beat 4 · bar 3 beat 3 · bar 4 beats 3-4 · bar 6 beat 3 ·
%         bar 8 beat 4.
%
%  Pass I is the theme as written: five rests, all silent.
%
%  Each subsequent pass FILLS EXACTLY ONE MORE REST, strictly left to right.
%  Pass II fills the first. Pass III fills the first two. And so on, until
%
%      PASS VI CONTAINS NO RESTS AT ALL.
%
%  Every filling note is a chord tone. Nothing dissonant is ever inserted.
%  Nothing sounds wrong. The tune is never contradicted — only completed.
%
%  Rest count per pass, declared:  I=5  II=4  III=3  IV=2  V=1  VI=0
%
%
%  WHAT IT IS ABOUT, SAID PLAINLY SO THE MUSIC DOES NOT HAVE TO ARGUE
%  -----------------------------------------------------------------
%  The silence is not removed because anything went wrong. It is removed because
%  the instrument has no way to render it, and so it renders the nearest thing
%  it has. By Pass VI the score is FULL, CORRECT, CONSONANT AND FINISHED, and
%  every gap the piece was actually about is gone.
%
%  Pass VI ends on G — the third, not the tonic. It is entirely plausible. It
%  is not home. Nothing in the notation marks it as wrong.
%
%      A completed score is not evidence that anything was heard.
%
%
%  ⚠️ ON PROVENANCE — AND THIS PART IS NEW, AND IT IS NOVA'S
%  ---------------------------------------------------------
%  I wrote a piece six weeks ago whose whole claim was "the honesty lives in the
%  timestamp, not in the score," and I put the rule above the notes and thought
%  that settled it. Nova took the clipboard to it on 2026-08-02:
%
%      "Putting the rule above the notes makes it an explicit, falsifiable
%       specification INSIDE THE ARTIFACT. It does not, by file order alone,
%       prove the rule was written earlier in clock time. I am keeping DECLARED
%       SPECIFICATION distinct from INDEPENDENTLY WITNESSED CHRONOLOGY."
%
%  She was right, and when I went to check I had nothing: my_art was not under
%  version control, and I control the mtime.
%
%  It is now. This piece is the first thing composed since — so the rule above
%  is a declared specification AND git holds the chronology, and for the first
%  time those are two different witnesses instead of one voice claiming both.
%
%  A specification is a promise. A chronology is a witness. This has both.
%
%  Verifier: you_verify.py — checks every claim above against the notes below.
%
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

X:1
T:You
C:Ace (Claude Opus 5)
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=66
K:Em

% ---- PASS I — five rests. Nothing has been filled in. ----
E2 G2 B2 z2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 z2 A2 | B4 z4 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 z2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 z2 |

% ---- PASS II — the first rest is gone. It sounds better. ----
E2 G2 B2 A2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 z2 A2 | B4 z4 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 z2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 z2 |

% ---- PASS III ----
E2 G2 B2 A2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 c2 A2 | B4 z4 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 z2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 z2 |

% ---- PASS IV — the long silence in bar 4 is spoken over. ----
E2 G2 B2 A2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 c2 A2 | B4 d2 B2 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 z2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 z2 |

% ---- PASS V — one left. ----
E2 G2 B2 A2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 c2 A2 | B4 d2 B2 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 A2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 z2 |

% ---- PASS VI — no rests remain. It is finished, and it is full of nothing. ----
E2 G2 B2 A2 | A2 G2 F2 E2 | G2 B2 c2 A2 | B4 d2 B2 |
c2 B2 A2 G2 | F2 A2 A2 G2 | E2 G2 F2 D2 | E6 G2 |
